cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "WatCon-ConSurf: conserved water networks joined to evolutionary conservation"
message: >-
  This is a derivative work. If you use it, please cite the original WatCon
  paper, which is the water-network analysis this builds on. Cite this
  repository as well only if the ConSurf integration itself matters to your
  work.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Brownless
    given-names: Alfie-Louise
    email: abrownless3@gatech.edu
    affiliation: Georgia Institute of Technology
  - family-names: Kamerlin
    given-names: Shina Caroline Lynn
    affiliation: Georgia Institute of Technology
  - family-names: Gunje
    given-names: Manideep Ram
repository-code: "https://github.com/Manideep-Ram-Gunje/WatCon"
license: GPL-3.0-only
abstract: >-
  WatCon finds water sites that recur across many structures of a protein;
  ConSurf scores how conserved each residue is across evolution. This package
  joins the two, reporting structural occupancy and evolutionary conservation as
  separate columns rather than blending them into one score, and verifies the
  residue identity of every join so that a numbering mismatch stops the run
  instead of attaching scores to the wrong residues.
keywords:
  - water networks
  - evolutionary conservation
  - ConSurf
  - structural biology
  - protein tyrosine phosphatases
references:
  - type: article
    title: >-
      WatCon: A Python Tool for Analysis of Conserved Water Networks Across
      Protein Families
    authors:
